c0fbed5797846a9f4.png)\n\nThe diagonals $XZ^(―)$ and $WY^(―)$ of the parallelogram $WXYZ$ shown above are congruent. You may assume that $m∠YZW + m∠XWZ = 180^{∘}$.\n\nNow, consider the following statement:\n\n> *$WXYZ$ is a rectangle.*\n\nThe table below gives the proof of the statement. Select the correct options in the table and thus complete the proof.\n\n|  | Statement | Reason |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | $WXYZ$ is a parallelogram | {{field-1}} |\n| 2 | $WX^(―)≅YZ^(―)$ | {{field-2}} |\n| 3 | $XZ^(―)≅WY^(―)$ | {{field-3}} |\n| 4 | $WZ^(―)≅WZ^(―)$ | {{field-4}} |\n| 5 | $△XWZ≅△YZW$ | {{field-5}} |\n| 6 | $∠XWZ≅∠YZW$ | {{field-6}} |\n| 7 | $m∠XWZ = m∠YZW$ | {{field-7}} |\n| 8 | $m∠YZW + m∠XWZ = 180^{∘}$ | {{field-8}} |\n| 9 | $m∠YZW + m∠YZW = 180^{∘}$ | {{field-9}} |\n| 10 | $m∠YZW = 90^{∘}$ | {{field-10}} |\n| 11 | $m∠XWZ = 90^{∘}$ | {{field-11}} |\n| 12 | $∠WXY≅∠YZW$, $∠XYZ≅∠XWZ$ | {{field-12}} |\n| 13 | $m∠WXY = 90^{∘}$, $m∠XYZ = 90^{∘}$ | {{field-13}} |\n| 14 | $WXYZ$ is a rectangle | {{field-14}} |"}] :knowledge-point/title "Constructing Complete Proofs of Parallelogram and Rectangle Criteria" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049237 :topic/math-academy-id 5169 :topic/title "Proving Criteria for Parallelograms and Rectangles"}]}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203986 :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204349 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186201076} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186201077} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186201078} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186201079} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186201080}] :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186201075}] :question/math-academy-id "q-225656" :question/problem "A bag contains $250$ red balls and $150$ white balls. We wish to construct a statistical model for $p^(^)$, the proportion of white balls in a random sample.\n\nConsider the following random samples.\n\n1. $30$ balls are drawn from the bag without replacement.\n2. $22$ balls are drawn from the bag with replacement.\n3. $15$ balls are drawn from the bag without replacement.\n\nWhich of these sampling methods requires a finite population correction factor to be applied to model $p^(^)$ as normal?\n\n*Hint: In all cases, the requirement $np > 5$ and $n(1 - p) > 5$ is satisfied.*"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204356 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186201187} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186201188} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186201189} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186201190} {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186201191}] :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186201186}] :question/math-academy-id "q-226014" :question/problem "In which of the following situations is it necessary to use a finite population correction factor to adjust the variance so that the sample proportion distribution is approximately normal?\n\n1. A sample of size $n = 40$ from a population of size $N = 1000$ in which $25%$ of the population has a particular characteristic.\n2. A sample of size $n = 500$ from a population of size $N = 3000$ in which $80%$ of the population has a particular characteristic.\n3. A sample of size $n = 150$ from a population of size $N = 2000$ in which $40%$ of the population has a particular characteristic.\n\n*Hint. In all cases, the requirement $np > 5$ and $n(1 - p) > 5$ is satisfied. You may assume all samples are conducted without replacement.*"}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Situations Where a Correction Factor Is Necessary" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049385 :topic/math-academy-id 5020 :topic/title "Finite Population Corrections for Sample Proportions"}]}]}
